@page "/components/popconfirm"
@page "/components/pop-confirm"

<PageOutlet Url="components/popconfirm"
            Title="PopConfirm"
            Description="BitPopConfirm asks for a confirmation right where an action is triggered, in a small popup anchored to the trigger." />

<DemoPage Name="PopConfirm"
          SecondaryNames="@(["Popconfirm", "ConfirmPopover", "ConfirmPopup"])"
          Description="BitPopConfirm asks for a confirmation right where an action is triggered: a small popup anchored to the trigger, with a question and the two buttons that answer it, for the actions worth a second thought that are not worth a modal dialog."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          SubEnums="componentSubEnums"
          CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          GitHubExtrasUrl="PopConfirm/BitPopConfirm.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="Extras/PopConfirm/BitPopConfirmDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            To use this component, you need to install the
            <BitLink Href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Bit.BlazorUI.Extras" Target="_blank">
                <BitTag Reversed
                        Text="Bit.BlazorUI.Extras"
                        Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground"
                        IconName="@BitIconName.NavigateExternalInline" />
            </BitLink>
            nuget package, as described in the Optional steps of the
            <BitLink Href="/getting-started">Getting started</BitLink> page.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            The popup is an alertdialog that keeps the focus while it is open, starting on the Cancel button, so an Enter
            pressed out of habit does not confirm. Every opening ends in exactly one of OnConfirm and OnCancel: Confirm
            raises the first, and Cancel, Escape and a click outside of the popup the second. The focus goes back to the
            trigger either way.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" Id="example1">
            <div>
                The trigger is the child content, and a click on it opens the popup. The title names the popup and the
                message describes it.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div>
                <BitPopConfirm Title="Delete the file?"
                               Message="It cannot be brought back once it is deleted."
                               OnConfirm="@(() => basicAnswer = "Deleted")"
                               OnCancel="@(() => basicAnswer = "Kept")">
                    <BitButton IconName="@BitIconName.Delete">Delete</BitButton>
                </BitPopConfirm>
                <br />
                <br />
                <div>Answer: <b>@basicAnswer</b></div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Message only" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" Id="example2">
            <div>Without a title, the message is what names the popup.</div>
            <br />
            <div>
                <BitPopConfirm Message="Discard the changes?">
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Discard</BitButton>
                </BitPopConfirm>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Button texts" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" Id="example3">
            <div>ConfirmText and CancelText say what the two answers do, which reads better than a plain Ok.</div>
            <br />
            <div>
                <BitPopConfirm Title="Leave the meeting?"
                               Message="The others will carry on without you."
                               ConfirmText="Leave"
                               CancelText="Stay">
                    <BitButton IconName="@BitIconName.Leave">Leave</BitButton>
                </BitPopConfirm>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Placement" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" Id="example4">
            <div>The popup takes the side of the trigger with the most room, unless Placement names one.</div>
            <br />
            <div>
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitPopConfirm Message="Placed on top." Placement="BitPlacement.Top">
                        <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Top</BitButton>
                    </BitPopConfirm>
                    <BitPopConfirm Message="Placed at the bottom." Placement="BitPlacement.Bottom">
                        <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Bottom</BitButton>
                    </BitPopConfirm>
                    <BitPopConfirm Message="Placed at the start." Placement="BitPlacement.Start">
                        <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Start</BitButton>
                    </BitPopConfirm>
                    <BitPopConfirm Message="Placed at the end." Placement="BitPlacement.End">
                        <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline">End</BitButton>
                    </BitPopConfirm>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Binding" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5">
            <div>
                IsOpen is two-way bindable. Opening or closing the popup through it is the page's own doing, and raises neither
                OnConfirm nor OnCancel.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div>
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitPopConfirm Title="Archive the project?" @bind-IsOpen="isBoundOpen">
                        <BitButton IconName="@BitIconName.Archive">Archive</BitButton>
                    </BitPopConfirm>
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Text" OnClick="() => isBoundOpen = true">Ask from here</BitButton>
                    <BitText>IsOpen: <b>@isBoundOpen</b></BitText>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Disabled" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" Id="example6">
            <div>A disabled pop confirm never opens, and leaves the trigger as it is.</div>
            <br />
            <div>
                <BitPopConfirm Title="Delete the file?" Disabled>
                    <BitButton IconName="@BitIconName.Delete">Delete</BitButton>
                </BitPopConfirm>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Color" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
            <div>ConfirmColor colors the Confirm button: Error marks an action that destroys something.</div>
            <br />
            <div>
                <BitPopConfirm Title="Delete the account?"
                               Message="Every project of the account goes with it."
                               ConfirmText="Delete"
                               ConfirmColor="BitColor.Error"
                               OnConfirm="() => accountDeleted = true">
                    <BitButton Color="BitColor.Error" IconName="@BitIconName.Delete">Delete account</BitButton>
                </BitPopConfirm>
                <br />
                <br />
                <div>Deleted: <b>@accountDeleted</b></div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" Id="example8">
            <div>
                Classes and Styles reach every part of the pop confirm, and the --bit-PopConfirm-* variables restyle its
                defaults, set on the Style of the pop confirm or on any of its ancestors.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div>
                <div class="example-content">
                    <BitPopConfirm Title="Styled popup"
                                   Message="With custom styles."
                                   Style="--bit-PopConfirm-title-color: tomato; --bit-PopConfirm-max-width: 14rem;"
                                   Styles="@(new() { Callout = "border-radius: 1rem;", Actions = "justify-content: center;" })">
                        <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Styles</BitButton>
                    </BitPopConfirm>
                    <BitPopConfirm Title="Classed popup"
                                   Message="With custom classes."
                                   Classes="@(new() { Callout = "custom-popconfirm", Message = "custom-message" })">
                        <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Classes</BitButton>
                    </BitPopConfirm>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example9RazorCode" Id="example9">
            <div>Use BitPopConfirm in right-to-left (RTL).</div>
            <br />
            <div dir="rtl">
                <BitPopConfirm Dir="BitDir.Rtl"
                               Title="فایل حذف شود؟"
                               Message="پس از حذف قابل بازگشت نیست."
                               ConfirmText="حذف"
                               CancelText="انصراف">
                    <BitButton IconName="@BitIconName.Delete">حذف</BitButton>
                </BitPopConfirm>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
</DemoPage>
